/*
[bifurcation]
parameter: K
min: 0.5
max: 6
ds: 0.002
dsMax: 0.02
maxSteps: 4000
*/

// ROSENZWEIG-MACARTHUR PREDATOR-PREY - the paradox of enrichment.
//
//   Run:  parameter K, range 0.5 : 6, plot N
//   Then: Curve = Hopf curve, second parameter m
//
// Logistic prey with a Holling type II (saturating) predator:
//
//   dN/dt = r N (1 - N/K) - a N P / (1 + a h N)
//   dP/dt = e a N P / (1 + a h N) - m P
//
// The interior equilibrium has N* = m / (a (e - m h)), independent of K, so
// enriching the system - raising the carrying capacity K - does not raise
// the prey level at all. Past a Hopf point it destabilises it instead, and
// the system starts to cycle. That is the paradox of enrichment, and it is
// one of the clearest cases where a bifurcation diagram says something a
// simulation at one parameter value cannot.
//
// Continuing the Hopf point in (K, m) shows where in the plane the cycling
// regime lives.

-> N; r*N*(1 - N/K) - a*N*P/(1 + a*h*N)
-> P; e*a*N*P/(1 + a*h*N) - m*P

r = 1
K = 3
a = 1
h = 1
e = 0.5
m = 0.2

N = 0.7
P = 0.5
